WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:03.060
- [Narrator] Sustainable, eco-conscious,

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.130
made from recycled materials.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.410
Companies are using phrases like these

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:09.870
more and more frequently today.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.560
Sales of consumer packaged
goods advertising sustainability

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:18.030
in North America rose by
over 30% in just five years,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:23.030
reaching an estimated
$268.9 billion in 2022.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:27.150
And more than 60% of
consumers say they'd pay more

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.760
for a product with sustainable packaging.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:31.770
But underneath the flashy buzzwords,

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:35.400
companies can fall short of
their eco-friendly promises.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.000
Environmental activists say an
ongoing legal battle with H&amp;M

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.880
might highlight a big
company's alleged greenwashing.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:44.760
That's when companies mislead
consumers with promises

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.240
of being more eco-friendly
than they actually are.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.330
In 2022, a class action lawsuit claimed

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:54.168
that the clothing retailer's
Conscious Choice collection

00:00:54.168 --> 00:00:56.970
advertised itself as sustainable,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.220
but it was actually not
made from sustainable

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.860
and environmentally friendly materials.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.450
The case is still ongoing,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.080
and H&amp;M says it's taking these
allegations very seriously,

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.700
is looking into them thoroughly,

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:10.410
and that its sustainability work

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.780
has never been about greenwashing.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.750
Here's how a lack of
transparency with consumers,

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:18.630
long timelines, and
weak environmental goals

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.886
can all be signals of greenwashing.

00:01:20.886 --> 00:01:24.210
(pensive music)

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:27.090
Greenwashing is when a
company or organization

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.910
uses misleading advertising
to market itself

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:33.300
as more environmentally
friendly than it really is.

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.940
Sustainable ESG funds or investments

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.250
that consider environmental, social,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:40.110
and corporate governance factors

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.420
have exploded over the past decade,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:47.010
exceeding $350 billion
in net assets in 2021.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.929
- It's becoming increasingly profitable

00:01:48.929 --> 00:01:50.700
for companies to be seen

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:53.220
as being beneficial to the environment.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.000
Consumers are increasingly
demanding sustainable products.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.370
Investors want to invest in companies

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.590
that have lower carbon footprints.

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:04.320
- [Narrator] So why do people
criticize greenwashing?

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.810
- When you buy from a
company that is engaging

00:02:06.810 --> 00:02:10.530
in greenwashing practices,
you are encouraging

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.090
that behavior to continue.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.920
Other companies will take note.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.350
- [Narrator] Some companies say
their sustainability efforts

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.290
are better than doing nothing,
but critics would argue

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:21.210
greenwashing involves weak goals,

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:25.050
lack of transparency with
consumers, and long timelines.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.600
Take one common corporate
environmental promise:

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.340
reducing carbon emissions.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.980
One way companies say they
reduce their emissions

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.290
is by purchasing carbon offsets.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.930
A carbon offset is an action or activity

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.720
that balances the
emission of carbon dioxide

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:42.870
or other greenhouse gases
into the atmosphere.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:46.140
It's a buyable, sellable,
and tradable commodity.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.320
In theory, when a company
buys a carbon offset,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.590
it finances an action that
removes greenhouse gas emissions

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:54.690
from the air, like planting trees,

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.300
or building renewable energy projects.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.670
This is supposed to
balance the overall amount

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:02.790
of carbon dioxide a company
puts into the atmosphere.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.560
- Carbon offsets and carbon credits

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.950
have become an incredibly
controversial mechanism

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.720
in the fight against climate change.

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.170
And activists and environmentalists claim,

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.190
and our reporting shows,

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.820
many of these projects
are generating credits,

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:21.300
too many credits and not really reducing

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.990
or removing emissions that
companies are paying for.

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:27.600
- [Narrator] Critics say
carbon offset goals are weak,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:30.210
and that companies aren't
transparent with consumers

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:31.950
about how they'll achieve them.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.110
While companies can claim
they're putting money

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.470
towards carbon offsets, the
offset's environmental impact

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.510
isn't always clear to consumers.

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:43.080
Plus, activists say companies
have decades-long timelines

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.610
to reach their goals.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:48.510
A company can promise to
achieve zero emissions by 2050,

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:50.670
but if it doesn't have intermediary goals,

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:54.240
it's unclear when it will
actually start reducing emissions.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.020
And time is essential for
reducing greenhouse gas emissions.

00:03:58.020 --> 00:04:00.660
That's because the United
Nations Paris Agreement

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:05.130
calls for global emissions
to be reduced by 45% by 2030

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.440
in order to keep global
temperatures from rising

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.290
by no more than 1.5 degrees Celsius.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:13.170
So who is policing greenwashing companies?

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.570
- When a company does
engage in greenwashing,

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:19.290
the negative outcomes for
them are quite limited.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.110
In the worst case, it might
be a fine at this point.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.530
- [Narrator] In 2022, Goldman
Sachs's asset management arm

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:28.830
paid millions after violating
SEC compliance rules

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.960
with ESG fund marketing.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.560
Goldman neither admitted nor
denied the SEC'S allegations,

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.540
but there's increased
efforts by governments

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.570
to start cracking down on
greenwashing companies.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:42.420
In March, the European
Union ruled out a series

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.610
of proposals, which
would require companies

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.010
to back up their products'
environmental claims

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.840
with scientific evidence.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.290
These claims would then need
to be independently verified.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.630
This is part of a larger
effort to make it easier

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.450
for consumers to choose
sustainable products.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:05:01.650
There are roughly 450 million
people in the European Union.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.780
Regulations adopted there often lead

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.430
to larger changes globally

00:05:05.430 --> 00:05:07.950
since multinational
companies prefer not to work

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.620
with different sets of
rules around the world,

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.990
and there's movement in the US as well.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.570
The Federal Trade Commission
is discussing rules

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.630
that would regulate
environmental marketing claims.

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:20.460
It also wants to penalize companies

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.250
for each time a product
uses deceptive language.

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.500
And the SEC is proposing new disclosure

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.170
and naming and requirements for ESG funds.

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.837
(pensive music)

